%% ================================================================ PREFACE
\section*{The work}

\initial{N}{unc scio vere} (‘Now I know for certain’) is the Introit for Saints Peter and Paul
(29 June). Medieval Polish graduals also give it to Saint Peter’s Chains
(1 August). Wacław set the antiphon and the \la{Gloria Patri}. The psalm
verse between them was sung to its chant tone, and the antiphon was then
repeated.

The setting survives in one source, an eight-leaf fragment of an organ
tablature of about 1590. Aleksander Poliński found it, and it is called the
‘Castle’ tablature after the State Art Collections in the Royal Castle,
Warsaw, where it was later kept. It passed to the National Library and was
destroyed in 1944. The fragment was probably part of the Łowicz tablature,
which contains a cycle of introits by Marcin Leopolita and others. \la{Nunc
scio vere} stood on fols.\,7v–8r, headed \la{In die Apostolorum Petri et
Pauli. Nunc scio vere W. S.} (‘On the day of the Apostles Peter and Paul.
Now I know for certain. W. S.’) The monogram is usually read as Wacław’s.
Tomasz Czepiel doubts the attribution.

Wacław studied in Poznań and Kraków. He became composer to Sigismund II
Augustus in 1547 and later served Mikołaj Radziwiłł ‘the Black’. Two other
Latin motets by him survive, both printed in Nuremberg: \la{In te Domine
speravi} (1554) and \la{Ego sum pastor bonus} (1564). A Kraków inventory of
1572, made after the death of Jurek Jasiuczyc, lists ‘offitia Vaczlavove’
(‘Wacław’s offices’).
Poles used \la{officium} for the introit, so these may have been settings
like this one.

\section*{The music}

The introit chant (third mode) runs through the setting in equal values,
lightly ornamented. It moves between voices. The Tenor has it for \la{Nunc
scio vere} (bars 3–12) and the Cantus from \la{quia} to the end of the
antiphon (13–56). In the \la{Gloria Patri} the Bassus recites the psalm tone
on c (63–71). These entries are marked [c.f.]. The other voices treat each
clause of text as a point of imitation. The antiphon ends on E, the final of
the mode. The \la{Gloria Patri} ends on A, the last note of the psalm tone’s
termination.

\section*{The reconstruction}

Mirosław Perz made a vocal version from photographs of the two pages and
published it in 1966 (\sig{P}). He set it a minor third above the tablature
(\sig{T}) and added his own underlay. His commentary records every reading of
\sig{T} that he changed. This edition starts from \sig{P}, returns the music
to the pitch of \sig{T} and weighs each of Perz’s changes against \sig{T}.
Most stand. At bars 6, 9, 30 and 81–83 \sig{T}’s reading is restored. The
critical notes list every decision, so any of them can be reversed.

\section*{Sources}

\begin{sigla}
\siglum{T} Organ tablature, c.\,1590 (‘Castle’ or ‘Small Warsaw’
  tablature; Poliński collection), fols.\,7v–8r: \la{In die Apostolorum
  Petri et Pauli. Nunc scio vere W. S.} Text incipit only. Destroyed 1944.
  Glass-plate photographs of three pieces from the fragment, probably of
  1928, are in the Institute of Musicology, Jagiellonian University
  (IMuzUJ 014–017).
\siglum{P} Mirosław Perz, vocal reconstruction from photographs of \sig{T},
  in Hieronim Feicht (ed.), \textit{Muzyka staropolska / Old Polish Music}
  (Kraków: PWM, 1966), pp.\,61–65; commentary pp.\,380–81. Up a minor
  third; values as in \sig{T}; editorial underlay. \textit{Base text,}
  revised against \sig{T}.
\end{sigla}

\section*{Editorial method}

\subsection{Pitch and note values} Pitch is that of \sig{T}. At this pitch
the antiphon ends on E with no signature, the normal position of the third
mode, and the voices fit the ordinary low clefs (C1, C3, C4, F4). Cantus
c′–e″, Altus f–a′, Tenor d–e′, Bassus E–a. A vocal original in this mode
would have been notated this way, and nothing suggests the intabulator
transposed it. \sig{P}’s pitch would need three flats in the signature,
which no source of the period uses. Written pitch was not sounding pitch,
though (see Performance).

Note values are twice those of \sig{T} and \sig{P}. German organ tablatures
often halve the values of their vocal models. As Perz transcribes it,
\sig{T} moves in semiminims and fusae, with semifusae at cadences, values a
vocal motet of about 1550 would hardly use. Doubled, the music moves in
semibreves and minims under ¢, like Wacław’s two printed motets. The bar is
a breve, so bar numbers match \sig{P}. The mensuration sign is editorial:
\sig{T}, a tablature, has none.

\subsection{Score} Bar lines run between the staves only. Voice names and
clefs are editorial. Each voice’s range is shown at the head of its staff.
Rests at entries follow \sig{P}, since a tablature notates rests only in
part. The antiphon ends on semibreves under \sig{P}’s corona (bar 56).
The \la{Gloria Patri} ends on longs.

\subsection{Accidentals} A tablature spells every pitch, so accidentals
before notes come from \sig{T}. Accidentals above the staff are editorial,
all of them suggested by Perz. Each sign applies only to the note it stands
against. An accidental in brackets above a note is optional: the note as printed is the edition’s reading, and the bracketed sign an alteration singers may make.

\section*{Text underlay}

\sig{T} gives only the text incipit. Every syllable below the notes is
therefore editorial, placed by the method below.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textit{Chant.} Where a voice carries the cantus firmus, it takes the
  chant’s syllables. Each syllable starts on the first note of its chant
  neume, and a chant melisma stays on one syllable, subject to the rules
  that follow.
\item \textit{Wacław’s practice.} His Kraków \la{Lamentationes} (1553) set
  most of the text one syllable to a note, repeated notes included. A
  melisma comes before the cadence on the stressed syllable of the last
  word, and the final syllable takes the cadence note (\la{mé–am, lú–cem,
  hó–minum, su-á–rum}). Short repeats are marked \la{ij}, longer ones
  written out. The Nuremberg prints of his motets agree at clause ends but
  place the words too loosely to settle more. Nine in ten clause-final words
  here follow the \la{Lamentationes}; the rest carry a short ornament or
  keep an imitative head-motif.
\item \textit{The theorists.} These rules are kept without exception. A new
  syllable goes only on a minim or longer note (Lanfranco 1533; Zarlino
  1558, IV.33). None goes on a dot, or on the semiminim after a dotted minim
  (Lanfranco, Zarlino). The first note after a rest and the last note of a
  phrase each take a syllable (Zarlino). No rest divides a word, and there
  is no elision (Vicentino 1555). A repeated note takes a new syllable
  unless it is a semiminim, one of Stoquerus’s necessary rules (c.\,1570,
  ch.\,15).
\item[] Other rules are applied with the latitude Stoquerus allows the older
  generation of composers, the Netherlanders whose idiom Wacław shares.
  After a run of semiminims the syllable usually changes on the second white
  note, not the first (Vicentino), but not always. Long or stressed
  syllables go to long notes (Zarlino). A clause is repeated to keep the
  voices together or for emphasis, and repeated whole (Stoquerus).
\item \textit{Accent.} Stress follows the penultimate rule as Kraków taught
  it in Wacław’s student years (Sebastian z Felsztyna 1518; Jerzy Liban
  c.\,1539). Hence \la{Dóminus, ángelum, erípuit, Heródis, exspectatióne,
  Iudaeórum, princípio, saeculórum.}
\item \textit{Imitation.} Each point of imitation takes its opening motif
  from a phrase of the chant, and the words of that phrase go with it. A
  motif keeps its words wherever it recurs. Perz used the same method for
  Leopolita’s introits from this tablature (1967).
\end{enumerate}

\noindent Repeated text is printed in italics, as editions print words
supplied for a source’s \la{ij}.

\section*{Performance}

At written pitch the setting suits an all-male chapel like Sigismund
Augustus’s, with boys or falsettists on the Cantus (c′–e″) and a low Bassus
(E–a). The chapel paid boy singers (\la{pueri, adolescentes cantores})
alongside its men, and Wacław’s \la{Lamentationes}, in clefs lower still,
were written for low men’s voices. Every voice lies two to five semitones
lower than in Wacław’s printed motets, the Cantus most of all. A minor third
higher, as in \sig{P}, the four ranges match his printed ones almost
exactly. That is the pitch for a mixed choir, and a sound choice for any
choir, since written pitch fixed only the relations between the voices.

A minim pulse of about 60–72 suits the declamation. The organ may double
the voices. The order is Antiphon, psalm verse (cantor, in chant), \la{Gloria
Patri}, Antiphon. The Introit was often sung in alternation with the organ.
Perz argued in 1967 that the \la{Gloria Patri} settings of Leopolita’s
introits in the same tablatures were organ versets, and that the organ could
play the repeat of an introit while the choir said the words quietly. This
edition texts the \la{Gloria Patri} because its Bassus recites the psalm
tone syllable by syllable. An organ doxology or an organ repeat of the
antiphon has as good a claim. Marcin Szelest has recorded the \la{Gloria
Patri} from \sig{T} as an organ piece (Accord, 2023).

Polish Latin suits the piece, with the sounds given in the series
pronunciation guide. Stress follows the penultimate rule (rule 4 above). The
guide’s \textit{tsi} for \textit{ti} before a vowel keeps the \textit{i} as a
syllable, as the underlay does: ex-spe-cta-ti-o-ne.

